{
	title: 'History generations: rewrites write a new file, appends keep an index',
	spec: `A session's history is a chain of generations, so rewriting it never makes a reader replay the whole file and an append never makes one rebuild its index. Restart with ~200 open tabs reads session.ason, marks.ason and history tails only.

Layout. sessions/<id>/ holds session.ason, history files, marks.ason and blobs/. The first generation is history.asonl (existing sessions keep theirs unchanged); later ones are history2.asonl, history3.asonl and so on. session.ason names the active generation and lists each one: file, reason (rebase, undo, clear, compact, fork, migrate), replaces (the generation it supersedes), continues (the generation, possibly another session's, holding the readable conversation before it, and the byte end it continues from), next (a floor for record numbers) and ts. Only the active generation is written; the others are frozen.

Append. One line to the active file, as before. marks.ason follows each append from that record alone: the processed-through offset, the offsets the tab state needs (question, answer, turn, turn end, latest prompt, latest reply, waiting inbox, transitions), the next record number, the changed-files count, and the active file's inactive byte ranges and dropped record numbers. Tab-list values (open question, asked) are read from those offsets; appends update the marks and never discard them. The marks are a cache: when missing, malformed, of another version, or naming another generation or a larger size, they are rebuilt by scanning the active file.

Amend in place. Editing the last prompt (/edit, task hx8), rewinding to an earlier prompt (task 26q) and undoing either stay in the active file. Old bytes stay; the amendment record (a quiet rebase record) and the replacement prompt are appended; the marks hold the dropped lines as inactive: from the target prompt up to the amendment, except lines that survive an amend (inbox, answer, notice, change). Readers skip inactive ranges without decoding them. Scanning the file alone reproduces the ranges.

Rewrite. /rebase, its undo and any other drop write a new generation holding the complete resolved records (rebases and edits applied, no rebase records, record numbers kept) of every generation from the earliest one the plan changes through the active one. Undo builds the restored state from the replaced generation plus every record appended since the rewrite; it never switches back to an old file. The undo rules of tasks 3zz, hx8 and 26q hold, including refusal when side-effect tools ran.

Boundary. /clear and compaction start a generation that continues the active one. Its first record is the reset or compact boundary (summary and kept prompts), followed by what must carry over: waiting inbox messages, results settling unfinished tool calls and pending transitions. Provider input starts at the last boundary in the readable chain, normally the start of the active file.

Fork. A /fork or subagent fork child's first generation continues the parent's generation at its byte end at fork time and sees it as it stood then: amendments the parent appends later do not apply. Nothing is copied.

Reading. Record numbers are unique and stable across a session's generations; a new record's number is past every number in every generation. Pages, links, READ_ENTRY, find, cost, stats, changed files and the context graph reach earlier generations through continues; READ_ENTRY and undo also reach replaced ones. Page positions name a generation and an offset; a client holding a position in a replaced generation reloads the snapshot.

Crash safety. A new generation's file is written and fsynced, then session.ason is written to a temporary file, fsynced and renamed, then the directory is fsynced; events go out after. A crash before the rename leaves the old generation active and an unlisted file that is ignored. The switch runs in one synchronous step that first checks that the active file has the size the rewrite read, or carries the records appended meanwhile into the new generation.

Migration. scripts/ holds an explicit, resumable conversion run with the host stopped: every session's marks.ason is rebuilt, and each history whose current records differ from its raw ones gets a resolved second generation (reason migrate) with the original kept as frozen generation 1 and its checksum recorded. It compares each session before and after (projected records and numbers, attachments, provider input, settings, inbox, open questions) and reports every difference.

Measured: restart request to a usable visible conversation with ~200 open tabs after repeated edits and appends: time, host memory, history bytes read and file opens.

Out of scope: deleting sessions and reclaiming space of frozen generations.`,
	status: 'planned',
	notes: [
		'Failure modes, ranked (silent first): 1) appends during a rewrite lost: checked size, carry-over. 2) record numbers reused after a rebase drops the newest records, so links point at the wrong record: next floor per generation. 3) a page cursor into a replaced generation returns wrong records: cursors name the generation. 4) a fork child sees amendments the parent appended after the fork: ranges come only from records before the continued end. 5) a crash mid-switch loses the new generation: fsync order. 6) find index offsets point into the old generation: its marks name the generation; a switch reindexes that session. 7) clear or compact drops waiting inbox messages or unsettled calls: explicit carry-over. 8) migration changes context silently: per-session compare report, originals kept. 9) running older Hal on a migrated home appends to frozen history.asonl and new Hal ignores it: reverting needs a reverse conversion. Dropped as out of scope: disk growth from rare rewrites.',
		'Amendments in place (step 3): a quiet drop-only rebase record triggers amendments.apply, which replays the file once (history.readSync + replay.current) and rewrites the turn marks over active records plus the inactive ranges; changed files are left alone because amended changes stay on disk. A non-amendment rebase or legacy replaces prompt sets marks.rebase and switches the file to the old whole-replay path for good, since later amendments are not tracked as ranges there. Marks version 3 forces a full rebuild; the migration must prebuild marks offline or the first restart scans every history. pages.ts hit the 20000-byte limit: turn-marks.ts and amendments.ts were split out.',
		'2026-11-09 user approved a worktree-only playable covering amend, rebase, undo, clear, compact and fork, with behavioral and performance tests; merge awaits review. Amendments overwrite only the affected tail with explicit inactive markers and append the replacement. No recovery journal or speculative disk-exhaustion protection. Rewrite indexes from a saved prefix state, not replay of prior amendments. Rebase generations retain applied display/undo markers rather than replayable plans. Fork freezes the parent generation before linking, because active files are now mutable. Repeated-amend latency is measured alongside restart reads. This supersedes the append-only amendment paragraph and the no-rebase-records wording in the initial spec.',
		'2026-11-09 phase 1 measured (server CPU, arm64; copy of a 23.6 MB session with 23,241 records, 24 legacy rebases, 26 compacts; base 4cb48739 / branch unmigrated / branch migrated): first start marks 4,608 / 2,078 / 749 ms; restart to visible conversation 3,832 ms 47 MB / 10 ms / 14 ms; prompt edit median 10,754 / 420 / 14 ms; /rebase dropping one record 28,577 / 21,151 / 2,136 ms; restart after rebase 13,630 / 10 / 10 ms. 233 open tabs (copy of a real home), base / migrated / unmigrated: first start tab states 13,704 ms 743 MB / 855 ms / 14,753 ms; restart all visible conversations 10,882 ms 407 MB / 2,039 ms 56 MB / 2,810 ms 185 MB; host RSS 1,787 / 90 / 1,624 MB. Benchmarks: tasks/e4z/history-edits.ts, tasks/e4z/restart-tabs.ts.',
		'rebase-rows build() looked up each group member with rows.find: quadratic, 9.1 s on the 23k-record session; a Map makes it 1.46 s. The first rewrite of an unmigrated legacy session bakes old divider texts, about 1.4 s per old rebase; migration removes that cost. Migration (scripts/migrate-histories, host stopped) rehearsed on a copy of 2,091 sessions (684 MB): 97 resolved, 0 differences, originals unchanged by checksum, 96 s CPU, +247 MB disk, slowest session 21.5 s. rewriteHistory.migrate sets replacesFile on the latest rewrite so /rebase undo still reverses it.',
		'Phase 2 deferred: clear and compact starting linked generations, fork linking instead of copying, and readers across the chain. They need pages, snapshots, find, READ_ENTRY and cost to page across files (pages.ts is at the 20 KB limit) and an audit of ~65 history.readSync callers. At stake: ~0.4 s whole-history parse and ~600 MB RSS per huge session at its first turn after restart; rebase copies only since the last /compact. Fork keeps copying.'
	],
	needs: [],
	foldInto: ['3zz', 'bq']
}
